.TH PROTOCOLS.CFG 5 "Version 4.3.30:  4 Sep 2019" "Xymon"
.SH NAME
protocols.cfg \- Configuration of TCP network services

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP "\fB$XYMONHOME/etc/protocols.cfg\fR"
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBprotocols.cfg\fR contains definitions of how
.I xymonnet(1)
should test a TCP-based network service (i.e. all common
network services except HTTP and DNS).
For each service, a simple dialogue can be defined to
check that the service is functioning normally, and optional
flags determine if the service has e.g. a banner or requires
SSL- or telnet-style handshaking to be tested.

.SH FILE FORMAT
protocols.cfg is a text file. A simple service definition for
the SMTP service would be this:
.br
.sp
.nf
\&   [smtp]
\&      expect "220" until "220 "
\&      send "ehlo xymonnet\er\en"
\&      expect "250" until "250 "
\&      options banner
.fi
.sp
The lines run in the order they are written, which is the order of the
conversation: wait for a greeting beginning with "220", send EHLO, check the
answer begins with "250". What the service said is
included in the status message.
.sp
Order is what a definition expresses. A server that speaks first is written
with \fBexpect\fR first; one that says nothing until asked \-\- clamd and
spamd \-\- with \fBsend\fR first. Sending before the greeting violates
SMTP, and Postfix 3.9 and later reject the session for it.
.sp
A definition may hold as many steps as the conversation needs.
.sp
For services that require ALPN protocol negotiation (such as IMAPS multiplexed
by Nginx on the same port as HTTPS), you can define:
.br
.sp
.nf
\&   [imaps443]
\&      options ssl,alpn=imap
\&      port 443
.fi
.sp
The full set of commands available for the protocols.cfg file are:

.IP "[NAME]"
Define the name of the TCP service, which will also be the column-name
in the resulting display on the test status. If multiple tests share
a common definition (e.g. ssh, ssh1 and ssh2 are tested identically),
you may list these in a single "[ssh|ssh1|ssh2]" definition,
separating each service-name with a pipe-sign.

.IP "\fBsend\fR \fISTRING\fR"
.IP "\fBexpect\fR \fISTRING\fR"
Defines the strings to send to the service after a connection is
established, and the response that is expected. Either of these
may be omitted, in which case
.I xymonnet(1)
will simply not send any data, or match a response against anything.

An \fBexpect\fR takes one line. Where a reply runs to several, say where it
ends:
.br
.sp
.nf
\&      expect "250" until "250 "
.fi
.sp
SMTP and FTP continue with "250\-" and finish with "250 ", NNTP ends a block
with ".", IMAP with the command tag. Naming the terminator reads to the end
of the reply however it is split across packets; without it the tail would be
matched as the answer to the next command.
.sp
A terminator ending in a space also matches that text at the end of a line,
so "250 " accepts a last line of "250" with no text after it. "250\-" does
not match either way.

The send- and expect-strings use standard escaping for non-printable
characters. "\er" represents a carriage-return (ASCII 13), "\en"
represents a line-feed (ASCII 10), "\et" represents a TAB (ASCII 9).
Binary data is input as "\exNN" with NN being the hexadecimal value
of the byte.

.IP "\fBstart\fR \fIFEATURE\fR"
Switch on something the CLIENT does. The word names code in xymonnet, not
anything sent to the server, and there are two: \fBtls\fR and \fBiac\fR.
Anything else is refused when the file is read.
.sp
\fBstart tls\fR upgrades the connection where the line sits, after the server
has agreed to it:
.br
.sp
.nf
\&   [submissiontls]
\&      expect "220" until "220 "
\&      send "ehlo xymonnet\er\en"
\&      expect "250" until "250 "
\&      send "starttls\er\en"
\&      expect "220"
\&      start tls
\&      send "ehlo xymonnet\er\en"
\&      expect "250" until "250 "
\&      port 587
.fi
.sp
Plaintext before the line, encrypted after it, on one socket \-\- what SMTP
on 25, submission on 587 and IMAP on 143 do, and the only way those ports
reach a certificate. The certificate is read from the upgraded session, so the
service reaches the \fBsslcert\fR column with its expiry checked.
.sp
As the FIRST step it is TLS from the first byte, which is what implicit-TLS
ports need: \fB[smtps]\fR, \fB[imaps]\fR and the rest are written that way.
.sp
Two words because the request is protocol-specific and the step is not: FTP
asks "AUTH TLS", POP3 "STLS", SMTP and IMAP "STARTTLS", and the asking is left
to a \fBsend\fR.
.sp
\fBstart iac\fR negotiates telnet options where the line sits:
.br
.sp
.nf
\&   [mud]
\&      start iac
\&      expect "Welcome"
\&      port 1701
.fi
.sp
IAC belongs to the telnet protocol, not to port 23 \-\- MUDs and BBSes
negotiate on their own ports. The commands are stripped off the front of the
reply, so the next step sees the text behind them, and they are refused:
\fBWILL\fR is answered \fBDONT\fR and \fBDO\fR is answered \fBWONT\fR,
which is what gets a server talking that holds its greeting until the client
replies. \fBWONT\fR and \fBDONT\fR are answered with nothing \-\- RFC 854
makes them the end of that option's negotiation, and replying starts an
exchange that never stops.
.sp
A 5250 or 3270 host sends no text at any point: once the options settle it
waits for a terminal to negotiate itself, which a probe is not. Such a service
can only be checked as a connect, and no version of Xymon has done more.

.IP "\fBport\fR \fINUMBER\fR"
The TCP port for this service, and the only place it comes from. Every entry
in the shipped file names one; an entry without it can only be tested where
hosts.cfg names a port for that host, and says so in the log rather than
being given one from elsewhere.
.sp
The host's /etc/services is not consulted. It was, for entries with no
\fBport\fR line, which meant a probe could be checking a port that differs
from machine to machine and that nothing in this file names.
.sp
A port may be given per host in hosts.cfg as \fBSERVICE:PORT\fR, which
overrides the entry.

.IP "\fBoptions\fR \fIoption1\fR[,\fIoption2\fR][,\fIoption3\fR]"
Defines test options. The possible options are
.br
.nf
\&   banner - read the socket once when no step asks to
\&   ssl - TLS from the first byte (the older spelling of a leading "start tls")
\&   telnet - negotiate telnet options (the older spelling of "start iac")
\&   alpn=protocol1,protocol2 - specify ALPN protocols to negotiate during SSL handshake
.fi
.sp
\fBssl\fR, \fBbanner\fR and \fBtelnet\fR are kept for files that already use
them; no shipped entry does. A step says the same thing and says where:
\fBssl\fR is a leading \fBstart tls\fR, \fBtelnet\fR is \fBstart iac\fR
(an entry carrying the option is given that step), and \fBbanner\fR is an
\fBexpect\fR \-\- which also asks for an answer, where \fBbanner\fR takes
the first read and judges nothing, so a service that accepts the connection
and never speaks reports up with an empty banner.
.sp
An entry with no \fBsend\fR and no \fBexpect\fR is a connect check: nothing
is asked and nothing is matched, so the port opening is the whole of it.
\fBldap\fR, \fBldaps\fR, \fBqmtp\fR, \fBqmqp\fR, \fBnetbios-ssn\fR and
\fBlpd\fR ship that way \-\- each waits for a length-prefixed binary request
whose reply a prefix match cannot judge \-\- and so does \fBbbd\fR, where
xymond answers only after the client half-closes the write side.
.sp
Such an entry reports up wherever the port is open, which is also what a
half-written one does. The file cannot tell them apart, so the shipped set is
pinned in the test suite rather than marked here.

.SH "WHAT CANNOT BE EXPRESSED"
An \fBexpect\fR matches the start of a reply and consumes to the end of the
LINE. Binary protocols work as a single exchange \-\- \fB[rdp]\fR ships one,
matching raw bytes written as \fB\\exNN\fR \-\- but not as a conversation:
when a step follows, the reply must end in a newline first, and binary framing
has none. Leave such a service its \fBport\fR and no steps rather than write
ones that will wait for a terminator that never comes.
.sp
Matching is anchored: \fBexpect\fR compares the start of the reply, and
\fBuntil\fR the start of a line. A token appearing anywhere else cannot be
reached. \fB[oratns]\fR, whose useful payload follows a length-prefixed packet
header, and \fB[ircd]\fR, where the 001 welcome is the second token on a line
beginning with the server's name, are both out of reach this way.
.sp
An empty \fBexpect ""\fR is the fallback: it matches any reply and rejects
none, so it cannot say whether the answer was the right one \-\- only that
the server sent one. Both entries above use it. It is worth having because a
step that only sends reports up against a port that accepts the request and
never answers, which is what a wedged server looks like from outside.
.sp
There is no branching either. A reply that may legitimately take one of two
forms \-\- \fBEHLO\fR answered with \fB250\fR, or with \fB500\fR by a
server that speaks only \fBHELO\fR \-\- cannot be written here.

.SH "WHEN SOMETHING CANNOT BE READ"
A line xymonnet cannot resolve refuses the whole entry: an unrecognised
directive or option, \fBstart\fR with a feature other than \fBtls\fR, a
second \fBstart tls\fR, \fBoptions ssl\fR together with \fBstart tls\fR,
or a mistyped \fBuntil\fR. It is reported when the file is read, and the
entry then sends nothing at all \-\- not even a TLS handshake.
.sp
Obeying the rest would report green: a dropped step leaves a shorter
conversation that still succeeds, and \fBoptions sssl\fR would ask for TLS
and get a plaintext probe. A refused entry is yellow with the reason in its
status, because the check is broken rather than the service.
\fB\-\-checkresponse=red\fR escalates it; unlike an \fBexpect\fR mismatch
it is reported whether \fB\-\-checkresponse\fR was given or not.
.sp
If the FILE cannot be opened, the same applies to every service: none is
checked, and each says so. xymonnet once fell back to a set of probes compiled
into the binary, so the columns kept reporting while the checks behind them had
quietly become older and weaker ones. What is still compiled in is the
name-to-port list described under \fBport\fR, and nothing else.

.SH FILES
.IP "\fB$XYMONHOME/etc/protocols.cfg\fR"
.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymonnet(1)

